  "diagnosis_text": "The failure is localised to **s3\u2019s flow table**, not a physical link or host attachment failure.\n\nEvidence:\n- `h1 \u2192 h5`: **100% ICMP loss**.\n- `h1 \u2192 h2` (same s3): succeeds.\n- `h6 \u2192 h5` (same s5): succeeds.\n- `h1 \u2192 h6` (across the network toward s5): succeeds.\n- All configured inter-switch links, including s3\u2019s uplinks and s5\u2019s links, are reported **ACTIVE**.\n\nOn **s3**, a high-priority flow installed by `org.coms6200.experiment` matches exactly:\n\n- Ethernet source: `00:00:00:00:00:01` (**h1**)\n- Ethernet destination: `00:00:00:00:00:05` (**h5**)\n- Priority: **50000**\n- Action: **NOACTION** (drop)\n- Counter: **8 packets**\n\nThis rule overrides the normal baseline forwarding rule for h5\u2019s MAC, which would otherwise send traffic from s3 toward the network on port 1.\n\nIt also explains why a reverse `h5 \u2192 h1` ping fails: h5\u2019s ARP request can reach h1, but h1\u2019s unicast ARP reply to h5 matches this drop rule, preventing resolution/return traffic.\n\n**Diagnosis:** an explicit high-priority drop flow on **s3** is blocking all h1-to-h5 Ethernet traffic (including ARP replies). The next corrective action is to inspect/remove or alter that experiment-owned flow/policy; I cannot modify it from here.",
